<BaseLayout title="San Diego, Again — Huanyu Chen"
  description="The city we keep driving back to: a Halloween panda at the San Diego Zoo, Christmas Day in LEGOLAND's Miniland, and an Easter balloon ride over the Safari Park.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2025</p>
  <h1>San Diego, Again</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">A panda on Halloween, a LEGO city at Christmas, a balloon at Easter, and counting.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>Three visits and one drive-through · October 2022 – April 2025</strong><br />
    San Diego Zoo (Halloween) · LEGOLAND California, Carlsbad (Christmas Day) · San Diego Zoo Safari Park,
    Escondido (Easter Sunday)<br />
    one giant panda, mostly hidden in bamboo · one toy red panda · a whole San Diego skyline in plastic bricks ·
    one tethered balloon · giraffes from the back of a truck
  </div>

  <p>
    Some places you visit once and tick off. San Diego is the one we keep going back to, and the reasons are not
    complicated. First, it's not a far drive. It is close enough to decide on in the morning and be back from at
    night, which is exactly the kind of distance that turns a city into a habit. Second, there is always something
    to see. There's the UC San Diego campus on its mesa, the zoos (there are two, and they are very different),
    the cliffs and coves of La Jolla, and the old adobe streets of Old Town, where the city began.
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, and maybe most of all, San Diego sits right up against the border, and you can feel it. The Mexican
    culture there is not a theme or a restaurant category. It's in the street names, the food, the music, the
    language you hear around you, and in the fact that another country is a twenty-minute drive away. Few
    American cities wear their neighbor so openly, and I like it for that.
  </p>
  <p>
    What follows is not one trip but several, told in order from the camera roll. Two of the visits were single days; for
    the zoo we drove down the evening before. Each one went to a different corner of the county. It's a holiday calendar, more or less: Halloween at the
    zoo, Christmas at LEGOLAND, Easter at the Safari Park. The map shows where they landed.
  </p>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-overview.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-overview.jpg'} alt="Map of San Diego County showing three visits: the San Diego Zoo near downtown, LEGOLAND in Carlsbad on the north coast, and the Safari Park inland near Escondido." /></a>
    <figcaption>Three visits, three corners of the county.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>October 2022: just passing through</h2>
  <p>
    The first time we drove this way, San Diego was only the road to somewhere else: we went straight through it
    to the border for a Day of the Dead weekend in Tijuana, which has <a href="/misc/tijuana-2022">its own
    story</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Halloween at the zoo · October 31, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    Two years later we came back for the city itself. We drove down the evening before Halloween and reached the
    city just as the sky over the downtown towers turned pink.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'downtown-dusk.jpg'} alt="The downtown San Diego skyline at dusk under a pale pink sky, seen from the freeway over low rooftops and palms." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Downtown from the freeway, the evening before.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The zoo was the next day. The <strong>San Diego Zoo</strong> sits in
    Balboa Park, a short hop from downtown, and has been there since 1916. It is one of the most famous zoos in the
    world, partly for its animals and partly for its setting: a hilly park of canyons and mesas planted so thickly
    that many of the paths feel like a botanical garden. The first thing you meet, a little after noon on a
    cloudless day, is a huge bronze lion in mid-leap over the entrance plaza.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zoo-lion.jpg'} alt="A large bronze sculpture of a leaping lion on the San Diego Zoo entrance plaza under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The welcome committee, 12:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The star of that autumn, though, was black and white. In the summer of 2024 the zoo welcomed two giant pandas
    from China, Yun Chuan and Xin Bao, the first giant pandas to arrive in the United States in more than twenty
    years, and they were a big part of why we went that day. A little after three o'clock we found one of them:
    Xin Bao, if I'm right. She was doing what pandas do best: sitting in tall grass with its back
    against a dirt bank, half buried in bamboo, and completely uninterested in the crowd. These pictures are about
    as good as it got. A rounded white back, a dark shoulder, and one black ear behind the leaves.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'zoo-panda.jpg'} alt="A giant panda sitting in long grass behind bamboo leaves against an earthen bank, partly hidden." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A panda, mostly bamboo. You have to want to see it.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    We had brought our own panda, too: a toy red panda that has travelled with us before and spent part of the
    afternoon being held up against the zoo's greenery, as if visiting relatives. Toys seem to like this zoo. Or
    rather, this zoo seems to like toys: the little hedgehog who was the mascot of our
    <a href="/misc/roadtrip-2024">2024 road trip</a> came with us that Halloween too, and this was the day we lost
    him for good. Somewhere in Balboa Park, a hedgehog is still on holiday.
  </p>

  <h2>Christmas in bricks · December 25, 2024</h2>
  <p>
    Eight weeks later we were back in the county, this time on Christmas Day and about thirty-five miles up the coast in
    Carlsbad. <strong>LEGOLAND California</strong> opened in 1999 as the first LEGOLAND outside Europe. It's a park
    built mainly for young children, but it has one corner that is really for everyone. At two in the afternoon on
    a grey, streaky-skied Christmas, the big yellow sun over the gate was the brightest thing around. Just inside
    stood a Christmas tree built entirely of LEGO, ornaments, candles, star and all.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'legoland-sign.jpg'} alt="The LEGOLAND California entrance sign with its yellow sun under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Christmas afternoon at the gate.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'legoland-tree.jpg'} alt="A tall Christmas tree built from green LEGO bricks with brick ornaments and a gold star." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Every needle a brick.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The holiday mood carried on in the details. One display case held a snowy winter village in miniature: chalets
    with frosted roofs, a little Christmas tree and a red steam train running along the front. It's the
    kind of scene people build at home on a dining table every December, except here somebody had the time and,
    presumably, a great many more bricks. It sat in a cabinet painted in candy-cane stripes, which
    seemed fair.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'winter-village.jpg'} alt="A glass display case of a LEGO winter village with snowy chalets, a Christmas tree and a red train." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A winter village, and a train to get there.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The real reason grown-ups come is <strong>Miniland USA</strong>: whole American cities rebuilt from millions of
    bricks at about one-twentieth scale, with water, boats and moving traffic. Washington, D.C. was the first city
    we reached. The Washington Monument, the Capitol dome and the Lincoln and Jefferson memorials are all there.
    In front of them, a red double-decker tour bus crosses a stone bridge over a reflecting pool that, on this
    cloudy day, reflected the real sky better than any model could.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-dc.jpg'} alt="LEGO model of Washington, D.C. with the Washington Monument, Capitol, memorials and a bridge over water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Washington, D.C., about knee-high.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    And then, a few steps on, San Diego itself, which made the day feel like a visit within a visit. The Miniland
    version of downtown has the Convention Center with its row of white sail-like peaks along the bay, the
    skyscrapers of the waterfront behind it, and, in its own oval of train track, Petco Park, home of the Padres.
    "SAN DIEGO" is printed on the outfield wall and the seats are packed with tiny fans.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-convention.jpg'} alt="LEGO model of the San Diego Convention Center with its sail-shaped roof, in front of downtown towers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Convention Center, sails and all.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-petco.jpg'} alt="LEGO model of Petco Park ballpark with San Diego and Padres signs, surrounded by downtown buildings and a toy train track." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Petco Park, sold out in plastic.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The best part of Miniland San Diego is the bay. Along the water there's a whole county fair in bricks: a Ferris
    wheel signed "San Diego Fair", stands for fried food on a stick, slushies and a chili cook-off, and hundreds of
    minifigures milling between them. Look past the wheel and the rest of the region is crammed onto the far
    shore. The stepped concrete tower on the hill is UC San Diego's Geisel Library, the famous building named
    after Dr. Seuss. To the right, the red roofs look like the Hotel del Coronado. It is, so far, the only UCSD
    in these photos.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-fair.jpg'} alt="LEGO county fair by the water with a Ferris wheel labelled San Diego Fair, food stands and minifigures, and a model of UC San Diego's Geisel Library across the water." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The fair, the bay, and Geisel Library on the hill behind.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Miniland goes on to New York, where the Empire State Building and One World Trade Center rise over a tight grid
    of brick avenues and Grand Central sits at the corner. Then, a little out of place in "Miniland USA" but
    nobody minds, comes London. Big Ben and the London Eye stand on a small island in a pond, framed by willows,
    with a giant stack of LEGO bricks and a brick elephant on the far bank. By half past three the light was
    turning gold.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-ny.jpg'} alt="LEGO model of Manhattan with the Empire State Building, One World Trade Center and Grand Central." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Midtown and the Financial District, a few steps apart.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'miniland-london.jpg'} alt="LEGO models of Big Ben and the London Eye on a small island in a pond, framed by trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A detour to London.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    We stayed until the park lights came on. At five to five, on the way out, the yellow sun over the gate was lit
    up against a deep blue dusk, the shops beneath it glowing. Within a few minutes the sky over the parking lot
    turned pink and orange behind the palms, which is about as Californian as a Christmas evening gets. It was a
    good way to spend a holiday: walking around the whole country, slightly scaled
    down, and ending up back in San Diego.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'legoland-dusk.jpg'} alt="The illuminated LEGOLAND California sign with its yellow sun at dusk." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lights on, 4:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Easter in a balloon · April 20, 2025</h2>
  <p>
    The zoo has a sister. The <strong>San Diego Zoo Safari Park</strong> opened in 1972 (it was called the Wild
    Animal Park until 2010) on some 1,800 acres of the San Pasqual Valley near Escondido, half an hour inland. Where
    the zoo is a dense garden, the Safari Park is open country, laid out so herds of African and Asian animals
    share big, rolling enclosures. We went on Easter Sunday, a bright spring afternoon. The first animal in the photos
    is a hornbill, perched high in a walk-through aviary with its orange casque against the netting.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'hornbill.jpg'} alt="A hornbill with an orange casque perched on a branch high inside a mesh aviary." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A hornbill, looking down on us.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The real plan was the <strong>Balloon Safari</strong>. It's a tethered helium balloon, yellow with red rays like
    a cartoon sun, that lifts a round open gondola straight up on a cable over the park and then winches it back
    down. From the launch pad it looks enormous. The balloon's skirt is printed with the zoo's Wildlife Alliance
    logo and the gondola is painted with animals. Underneath, it's all sky and steel cable.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'balloon-sign.jpg'} alt="The yellow Balloon Safari balloon floating above trees beside its round launch pad and sign." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The balloon, seen from the path.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'balloon-gondola.jpg'} alt="Looking up from beside the round gondola at the yellow and red balloon and its cables." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Boarding, more or less.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Standing in the gondola, you look straight up into the middle of it. The red and yellow stripes radiate out
    from a dark valve at the center, with the maker's name, Aérophile, printed around it and steel cables running
    down to the rim. It looked less like a balloon than a giant beach umbrella, and we were the handle. Then the
    winch let out the cable and the ground quietly fell away. There is no burner, since it's helium, so the
    ride up is a quiet one.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'balloon-under.jpg'} alt="Looking straight up into the underside of the yellow and red striped balloon, with cables converging." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Under the umbrella.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The best view was straight down through the netting. The whole park spread out below: winding paths, the
    pale roofs of the buildings, a turquoise pool, the green slopes of the enclosures. Right in the middle sat
    the perfectly round, dark shadow of our own balloon, with a tiny dot hanging under it that was us. It is the
    photo from that day I like most, a picture of ourselves without anyone in it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'balloon-shadow.jpg'} alt="Aerial view straight down through gondola netting onto park paths and enclosures, with the round shadow of the balloon in the middle." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>That shadow is us.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Looking out instead of down, the enclosures that seem huge from the ground resolve into a landscape. Green
    hills are crossed by pale dirt tracks, clusters of palms stand around watering holes, and the dry chaparral
    hills of inland San Diego roll on behind. From the air the African plains look surprisingly like the real
    thing, or at least like the documentaries. The giraffes standing in the grass were only specks, but at least they were there.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'plains-aerial.jpg'} alt="Aerial view over the Safari Park's plains enclosures: green slopes, dirt tracks, palm groves and a pond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The plains from above, 2:56 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Later in the afternoon we went into those same plains at ground level, on a safari truck. That's the other
    way to see this park, and the opposite of the balloon in every sense. Antelope grazed on the hillside right
    beside the track and barely looked up. Further on, giraffes and a
    rhino stood around a tall rock tower under the palms, and we watched them from the truck as it rolled
    past. Two hours earlier we had seen this ground
    from the balloon; now it was at eye level.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'antelope.jpg'} alt="Antelope grazing on a grassy hillside beside a dirt track in the Safari Park." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Neighbors on the hillside.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'giraffes.jpg'} alt="Giraffes and a rhino around a tall rock tower under palm trees on the savanna." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Giraffes, seen from the safari truck.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Next time</h2>
  <p>
    Look at the list at the top of this page and you'll notice that most of it is missing from the pictures. The
    photos so far have found a panda, a LEGO county fair and a balloon. They have not found La Jolla's sea lions and
    sandstone cliffs, Old Town's adobe plazas, or the real Geisel Library rather
    than the brick one. Those belong in this story too, and they will get their sections here. Which is the nice
    thing about a city that's not a far drive. There's always a next time, and it is never very far off.
  </p>
